> The 2 areas where AdvFS has improved when a domain is created on a v5.*
> system is that Directories have a btree structure for faster lookup,
> create, delete of huge directories (and I do mean huge).
>
> The 2nd important change was the way the AdvFS BMT metadata file was
> managed.  On domains created prior to Tru64 UNIX v5.*, it was possible
> to run out of extent map entries for the BMT.  On v5.* systems, this is
> no longer possible.

This was a point of bitter disappointment for me. In 4.0D one can issue
specific parameters for AdvFS domain creation in order to set the
pre-allocation size of BMT and an extent size. The man pages also give admin
a pretty good idea what these values should be for a particular number of
files. This really saved my day with our proxy cache, which ran at 170,000
files. Applying appropriate values changed my system from "unusable" to
"comfortable" state of operations.

In 4.0F they (Compaq) "fixed it". The original problem for them was not the
speed, but the possibility of running out of available BMT extents, thus
being unable create more files although you had space. Agreed, that is a
problem. They fixed by allowing for a greater number of extents and IGNORING
those domain creation parameters!

So, my proxy cache on 4.0F would not run out of BMT (it didn't run out on
4.0D either, it was just dead slow), but there was no way to speed it up.
Imagine my fury. Immediately, I switched back to 4.0D.

What is the situation with 5.0 family? Are they speedy when a domain has a
substantial number of files >150,000.

Nixie.




