X substantial number of files >150,000.
X 
X Nixie.

If you are talking about the mkfdmn/addvol -p and -x option, they still 
exist in v4.0f and while they are not longer needed, they exist in v5.*

Historically, the first changes to help avoid running out of BMT space 
was the addition of the -p and -x options to mkfdmn and addvol.  That 
was somewhere in the v4.0 time frame (maybe even back in the v3.2 time, 
not sure).

Then in v4.0d, AdvFS implemented a hold back of 20% of the disk for use 
as possible BMT storage.  If user data needed more than the 80% 
remaining, then the hold back area was given over to user data files, 
but while it was held back it allowed the BMT to grow in a contiguous 
manor which allowed the BMT to use fewer of its limited number of extent 
map entries.

I do not know of any changes in the v4.0f time frame related to the BMT.

In v5.0, a new on-disk format was impemented that allowed the BMT to 
have an unlimited number of extents so that it would no longer hit a 
limit.

But if you still want to use the -x and -p mkfdmn/addvol options, they 
still exist even in the most recent release.  They are even still 
documented.

                                        Bob Harris


