Roman Prokhorov <roma@stalker.gamma.ru> writes:

> Thanks for the tip about async safe functions. However it didn't help. 
> 
> What's wrong now? I don't use printf in the thread at all, but fork() 
> still doesn't invoke unless I call sleep(). 

Which is why fork()ing multithreaded executables is a Bad Idea (TM).

The second thread is stuck, trying to acquire the stdin lock:

    >0  0x3ff805b0400 in pthread_mutex_tryrec(...) in /usr/shlib/libpthread.so
    #1  0x3ff805afe1c in __pthread_mutex_trylock(...) in /usr/shlib/libpthread.so
    #2  0x3ff801bf1b4 in __flockall(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so
    #3  0x3ff801467ec in __libc_locks_reinit(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so
    #4  0x3ff800cf740 in __fork(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so
    #5  0x120001978 in func(pData=0x0) "forkthread.c":10
    #6  0x3ff805c7c78 in __thdBase(...) in /usr/shlib/libpthread.so

Who is holding the stdio lock? But the initial thread, of course:

    >0  0x3ff800cc9c8 in __read(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so
    #1  0x3ff8014287c in __read_nc(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so
    #2  0x3ff800d05cc in __filbuf(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so
    #3  0x3ff800d0780 in fgets(...) in /usr/shlib/libc.so    ## this is where the lock was
                                                             ## most likely acquired
    #4  0x120001a70 in main() "forkthread.c":30
    #5  0x120001888 in __start(...) in a.out

So, your choices are:

  - avoid forking [save yourself and future maintainers some grief]

  - postpone doing anything in the main thread until you know that the
    other thread *has already forked* [this is easy to do in your
    test program, using condition variable; but I suspect in your
    real app you just want to be able to fork, while other threads 
    are fread()ing]

  - fork a "slave process" before creating any threads, open a pipe
    to it. When the main process wants to fork(), have it write a
    message to the pipe to slave instead. The slave [which does
    not create any threads ever], can fork any time it wants to.

Cheers,
-- 
In order to understand recursion you must first understand recursion.


