Tru64.org and openvms.org are a good deal quieter than they used to be,
apparently largely because the forums have been redefined to avoid many
kinds of discussions (both technical - e.g., the relative merits of current
and future hardware platforms - and non-technical - e.g., the behavior of
the parent corporation) that had until recently spiced them up.  If the rest
of the material at the sites had changed similarly that would at least be
consistent.  However, Ken still trots out articles of all types that might
be of interest to his former readership, but now provides no forums in which
to discuss them.

For example, he just posted the HP press release about new Superdome 'world
records' (thinly disguised as a Business Wire article, but with the HP
disclaimer at its end) in tru64.org, which really begs responses pointing
out that the TPC-C result is not any kind of world record at all, at least
not at the tpc.org site, which still lists the Fujitsu SPARC-based
PRIMEPOWER entry at the top of the TPC-C non-clustered list, with the new
Superdome entry firmly in second place.  True, the Fujitsu box uses 128
processors and the Superdome only 64, but then the IBM Regatta nipping at
Superdome's heels in third place uses only 32, so that's hardly a relevant
factor in Superdome's favor - though the article acts as if it were later
when comparing Superdome's per-processor performance to SunFire's in the
TPC-H area, even though the difference is far less dramatic there.
Similarly, the article points out the marginal cost/performance advantage
Superdome enjoys over Regatta in TPC-C, but fails to point out the marginal
advantage SunFire enjoys over Superdome in TPC-H.

And in openvms.org he posted another thinly-veiled HP press release about
its recent financial performance, which nearly completely glosses over the
massive loss (over $2 billion) in the just-completed quarter by emphasizing
'pro forma' (and unaudited) results that simply keep sweeping expenses under
a rather arbitrary rug until the fudged numbers work out the way HP would
have liked them to.  The one reference to *actual* performance both fails to
identify the dollar amount of the loss and presents the per-share loss with
the notation 'GAAP':  one might easily believe that *that* was the result
that had only some kind of nominal, rather than substantive, significance if
one did not know that GAAP stands for 'generally accepted accounting
practices' - i.e., the way results are *supposed* to be calculated.

To Ken's credit, he also provides pointers to articles less favorable to HP.
But for sites that claim to be 'forums' for those interested in the two
operating systems, they seem far more 'read-only' now than that
characterization used to suggest.  Since they don't even provide a means for
reader feedback (at least that I could find), I'm providing it here.

- bill





