Except that Dave left out a bit.

"gzip -c" produces stdout output. But it's trying to *compress*
filename.tar.gz. It's the "gzip -dc" that says to *oncompress* the file.

Most of us use "gzcat filename.tar.gz | tar xf -", where gzcat is a shortcut
name for "gzip -dc". Others of us use the GNU version of tar from the RPM
package from Compaq and use "gtar -xf filename.tar.gz". The GNU version is
generally wildly superior to the default version on almost every UNIX
variant because of its internal handling of these sorts of things, and its
better behavior when descending a directory and failing to read a single
file.




