Nico Kadel-Garcia wrote:

> Most of us use "gzcat filename.tar.gz | tar xf -", where gzcat is a
> shortcut name for "gzip -dc". Others of us use the GNU version of tar
> from the RPM package from Compaq and use "gtar -xf filename.tar.gz".
> The GNU version is generally wildly superior to the default version on
> almost every UNIX variant because of its internal handling of these
> sorts of things, and its better behavior when descending a directory
> and failing to read a single file.

GNUtar has one serious limitation which can occasionally cause problems.  
It cannot deal with a filename (including path) of more than 100 
characters.

Not that I do not use it.  That utility is one of the first I install on 
a real Unix machine ;-)

Also, "gtar -xf filename.tar.gz", unfortunately will not de-compress the 
file and extract it.  You must use gtar zxf filename.tar.gz.  No dashes 
required either.



