In article <ajjb82$rbe$1@nsnmrro2-gest.nuria.telefonica-data.net>, "laredo" <cesar_bravo@eresmas.com> writes:
>
>i have a es40 with 1 gb and I upgrade to 4 gb, any recommedation about to
>change kernel parameter?

There's nothing you have to change.  Without any hint at all about
how the system is used, it is hard to make specific recommentations
for things you might want to change.

If the system was paging too much at 1 GB and your hope is that it
will page less at 4 GB, then you probably don't need to change
anything.  If the goal is to allow users to run processes with
more than 1 GB of data space (without necessarily paging too
much), then you'll need to change the per-process maximum data
size limit.  If you just want more memory for the file system
buffer cache, it will use whatever is free.

If the goal was to allow more virtual memory use, you didn't need
more physical memory for that; just increase the amount page/swap
space and the relevant per-process limits.  The additional real
memory, will just keep the paging from being as bad.

If you have a specific application that would benefit from the
additional memory, then knowing what that applications is would
be useful information.

