You might want to check using ssh/scp instead.

> It's not foolproof, but I'm not worried if the cluster switches hosts since
> I'll know that.  I may have an id or two that in not synched due to the
> timing of the file copy, but that is a very minor issue.
> 
> My question is: can I copy the password file without a problem?  I know I
> should use vipw to edit the file and have it maintained, which got me
> wondering if I could overlay the /etc/passwd file or do I need to do
> something special?

I'd recommend doing something special.  Think about crashes (on either
system) during the copy.  Or an accidently-filled root filesystem on
the copied-to system.

You want to make the switchover from old to new
versions of the file as atomic as possible, I think. Here's
what we do:

    copy the new file to /etc/passwd.NEW
    link this to /etc/ptmp (this should lock the existing passwd file)
    unlink /etc/passwd.OLD (if it exists)
    link /etc/passwd to /etc/passwd.OLD
    rename /etc/ptmp to /etc/passwd
    run /usr/sbin/mkpasswd on /etc/passwd

You probably should check each step for errors and bail if you
detect any. 

As another poster pointed out, NIS is an option. [We did the
above after giving up on NIS, though.] 


