On 12 Aug 2002 13:17:15 -0700, magyar wrote:

> We have a Dec Alpha file server on our network that lately, has been
> experiencing severe system lag at various times during the day.

Intermittent problems are always a challenge.  You have to start by
isolating whether the problem is on the host or on the network, because
your next steps will differ based on that...

Try 'ntop' (sorry I don't have a pointer to it, but a quick search on
Freshmeat.Net should find it for you) to see if you can get a feeling
for what's happening from that system's view of the network.

> it also is exremely slow when typing in commands from the console.

That _suggests_ the problem is on the host itself...

> Any ideas or other commands I could run to get more of a clue?

See if vmstat or monitor (ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/DEC/monitor.alpha.Z
or at least that's where I found a copy back in 1999) can give you some
insight that top isn't.  '/usr/sbin/swapon -sv' will tell you how much
of your swap space is in use (perhaps helping you determine if the
problem is one of a shortage of virtual memory, causing the system to go
into a "thrashing" state).

If you find and post more detail, I'm sure folks on the newsgroup will
try to help...

I hope that helps...

