OSF/1 as runs on Digital/Compaq/HP Alphas CPU's is the same Operating 
system as Digital UNIX, and Tru64 UNIX.  OSF made the sources for the 
core technologies available to several companies, but I think that only 
Digital Equipment Corporation actually delivered a line of products 
based on it (then again, what do I know, I live in a small cube and 
stare at a monitor all day :-)

Originally Digital called the Operation system OSF/1.  I think Digital 
started with v1.2, then v2.*, v3.* (there are most likely some between 
versions from 1.2 up to v4.0, I just don't know what they are).  v4.0 
had a collection of a, b, c, d, e, f, g letter releases, then v5.0, 
v5.0a, v5.1, and v5.1a (and I'm not sure, but v5.1b may be out there or 
real soon now; again it is that isolated life in a cube affecting my 
reality :-)

I think that somewhere during the 3.* or v4.* series, the name was 
changed to Digital UNIX.  Then there was talk during the v4.* days of 
OEM'ing the Operating system to anyone's IA64 platform, so the name was 
changed to Tru64 UNIX so that other vendors could put their name in 
front of it.  But that never happened.  Today it is called "HP Tru64 
UNIX"

I do not pretend to know the real truth or a proper timeline.  This is 
just what I think I remember of its history.  I was working on OpenVMS 
when it started.  I didn't get involved with Tru64 UNIX until I think 
v3.2 (there I remembered a between version :-)

                                        Bob Harris


