Are you sure that 4.0F is supported on that hardware?

If so then this procedure should work...

For safety, Take a backup and then remove the 5.1 disk from the machine.

Perform a normal install to the other disk.

Put your 5.1 disk back in.

You can then boot whichever one you like from the console >>>
prompt.

Simply use
>>> boot DEV

where dev is something like dka100 depending on the controller
and scsi id's of the disks.
If you find that the machine automaticaly boots you into 5.1 on
powerup then you will need to set the console environment variable
auto_action to halt, next time you power cycle the machine it
will stop at the console prompt and you can boot whichever os you
want.
It may be a good idea to do this when you are doing the 4.0f install.

Hope this helps
Adam
PS Check that the other disk is really spare, is it being used for
swap or as a mirror of the first disk? If so then the first time you
boot 5.1 again the 4.0f installation will be gone.





