On Thu, 1 Aug 2002 07:38:25 +0200, Nikola Milutinovic wrote:

> It is not overwriting /usr/bin/su, so permissions are irrelevant.

Sorry.  You seem to have misunderstood my point of removing read
permissions from setuid binaries:  On some systems, core dumps follow
symlinks.  If `pwd`/core is a symlink to (say for example) /etc/nologin,
a malicious use could very easily force the sysadmin to spend a great
deal of time in the machine room logging in to the host at the
console...

If a process cannot read the file which it is executing, that process
will not produce a core dump.

