It is probably vm address space needed by the compiler (memory leak
somewhere ?)

Check your own limits :
csh> limit
or
ksh>ulimit -a
default and max values are in proc: subsystem in /etc/sysconfigtab,
and in vm: maxvas parameter as well in version 4.

# sysconfig -q vm | grep -i vas
# sysconfig -q proc | grep -i size

If max values are enough, adjust the your shell values (inherited by
all child processes)
csh> limit datasize unlimited
csh> limit addresspace unlimited
and possibly
csh> limit stacksize unlimited

or

ksh> ulimit -a
will tell you default
ksh> ulimit -aH
will tell you max values
ksh> ulimit -d <maxvalue> -s <maxvalue> -v <maxvalue>

check result
ksh> ulimit -a

if max values are not enough, patch proc and vm ( in V4 ) subsystem -
/etc/sysconfigtab- and reboot

In both shells, do not care with memory (real memory) it is not
enforced at all in version 4, and not inforce by default in V5.
man csh / man ksh for limit / ulimit description
man sys_attrs_vm / man sysattrs_proc for sysconfigtab parameters desc.

Cheers,

Emmanuel


